gm-method/rendering (a three.js mesh that issues a CUSTOM gl draw from onBeforeRender to draw a subset of its geometry -- multiDraw, manual drawElements, an own element/vertex buffer -- produces GL 'Insufficient buffer size' / GL_INVALID_OPERATION storms on strict drivers (ANGLE/D3D11), and when it does draw: origin-collapsed verts, wrong normals/uvs, double-draw z-fight): ROOT CAUSE is bind timing. three's renderObject() calls object.onBeforeRender() BEFORE renderBufferDirect()->bindingStates.setup() binds the mesh's VAO, so any custom gl draw in onBeforeRender runs against stale/wrong element+vertex state. Owning just the element buffer fixes the GL error but desyncs from three's vertex attribs -> spikes; replicating the full VAO in the lib is fragile and broke FPS+raycast. The CLEAN three-native fix is geometry.groups: clearGroups() then addGroup(start_in_ELEMENTS, count_in_elements, materialIndex) per visible sub-range, and let three's NORMAL pipeline draw them -- correct VAO, correct attributes, no double-draw, no per-frame buffer churn, and Mesh.raycast stays intact (it walks the full index, not groups). PAIRED requirement: geometry.setDrawRange must be (0, Infinity) -- three only draws groups WITHIN drawRange and raycast is bounded by it, so a small sentinel drawRange hides all groups and breaks picking. Witness across a camera sweep: gl.getError 0, screenshot solid (not spikes), raycast hits, FPS restored. The GL error storm itself degrades the context (FPS collapse) on AMD/ANGLE -- a 'just 1 benign error left' is fine, but a storm is the FPS bug, not a cosmetic one.